Aim To implement a C++ program using default, parameterized and overloaded constructors to create library book records. To understand constructor delegation, member initializer lists and validation of object data.
Theory A constructor is a special member function that is automatically called when an object is created.
Types of constructors used:

Default Constructor – Initializes an object with default values.
Parameterized Constructor – Initializes an object with user-provided values.
Overloaded Constructors – Multiple constructors with different parameter lists.
Constructor Delegation – One constructor calls another constructor in the same class. A member initializer list initializes data members before the constructor body executes.
Algorithm
Start the program.
Define a class named Book.
Declare title, author and number of copies as private members.
Create a default constructor.
Create a title-only constructor.
Create a parameterized constructor.
Use constructor delegation to avoid repeated initialization.
Validate the number of copies to prevent negative values.
Create objects using different constructors.
Display the book details.
Stop the program.
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <stdexcept>
using namespace std;

class Book {
private:
  string title;
  string author;
  int copies;

public:
  // Parameterized constructor
  Book(string t, string a, int c)
      : title(t), author(a), copies(c) {
      if (copies < 0) {
          throw invalid_argument("Copies cannot be negative");
      }
  }

  // Default constructor using delegation
  Book() : Book("Untitled", "Unknown", 0) {}

  // Title-only constructor using delegation
  explicit Book(string t) : Book(t, "Unknown", 0) {}

  void display() const {
      cout << "Title: " << title << endl;
      cout << "Author: " << author << endl;
      cout << "Copies: " << copies << endl;
      cout << "-------------------" << endl;
  }
};

int main() {
  try {
      Book b1;
      Book b2("C++ Programming");
      Book b3("Object Oriented Programming",
              "Bjarne Stroustrup", 10);

      cout << "Default Constructor:" << endl;
      b1.display();

      cout << "Title-only Constructor:" << endl;
      b2.display();

      cout << "Parameterized Constructor:" << endl;
      b3.display();

      // Testing negative copies
      // Book b4("Invalid Book", "Unknown", -5);

  }
  catch (const invalid_argument& e) {
      cout << "Error: " << e.what() << endl;
  }

  return 0;
}
